# vibekit/web/static/style.css bundle manifest
# Files concatenated in declared order at Docker build time.
#
# Slicing follows the existing @layer cascade:
#   00-02  layer declaration + @property defs (00), @layer tokens (01),
#          @layer reset (02); the ui-primitives base and 04-uip-skin sit
#          unlayered between 01 and 02
#   03     base — UNLAYERED, deliberately (see 10-29)
#   10-29  feature stylesheets, split by feature (chat, tools, input, ...).
#          Only 10-shell-app.css is @layer components — it opens the block on
#          line 1 and closes it on its last. The other 21 are UNLAYERED, so they
#          beat it and every layered rule, and a tie between two of them is
#          decided by the order below. That is why 15-input.css takes the
#          `transition` property from 03-base.css's press rule.
#
#          THAT ONE-MEMBER LAYER IS LOAD-BEARING, which is not obvious and cost
#          a packet to establish. `components` ranks BELOW `utilities` and
#          `mobile` in the declaration above, so 50-mobile.css's overrides beat
#          10-shell-app.css whatever their specificity. Unlayering it would put
#          it above every layer and flip three pairs, all mobile regressions:
#          #sidebar width+z-index (the drawer stops being full-width and drops
#          behind other chrome), .tab-close width+height (44px touch target
#          falls back to 24px) and .sidebar-close-only display (the drawer can no
#          longer be closed). It was FOUR until the dead #user-popup pair went
#          with the old sidebar popup it styled. Enumerate with
#          `python3 scripts/css-cascade-audit.py` before touching this.
#   30     @layer utilities
#   40     a11y (between utilities-close and mobile-open)
#   50     @layer mobile
#   60-61  MCP integrations (live in implicit outer layer, post-mobile)
#   70     selection — the one selected-state treatment for every surface that
#          has one. LAST on purpose: each of its selectors carries real
#          specificity, so coming after every feature file is the only thing
#          that lets it beat their equal-specificity `:hover` rules. It
#          therefore owns the selected hover and press too.
